A||||7||mbramble||Fascia Repair Question||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||03-02-2001||11:18 PM||||What can I use, and where can I get, whatever is needed to repair tears in the fascia??<P>Someone pointed me to <A HREF="http://www.fusor.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fusor.com/</A> <BR>and it looks like they have the right stuff but there are no distributors near me.  Is there anything else that I can use that I might be able to purchase locally??  I have two tears, each about 6-8" long on my front fascia from a wreck last week.  I would like to fix them and get the car back to looking good.<P>Any help is greatly appreciated.<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||PJ||03-02-2001||11:34 PM||||You need a two part flexible urethane repair cartridge. Fits in something that looks like a small caulking gun except two cylinders instead of one. Mixes the two parts in the tip as they are extruded. Get extra tips, this stuff kicks off FAST. Any paint and body supply shop should have them and the instructions.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||03-02-2001||11:36 PM||||Mike,<P>You should be able to get what you need at any parts store that has a good body repair section. Look for 'Flexiable bumper repair kits'. I'm not sure what you have in FL, but there should be a Car Quest store near you. That's where I get my stuff, unless I obtain it from Military sources.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroLisa||03-03-2001||12:27 AM||||just be careful, if you ever bump the front end on anything, your patch up job won't stand up to it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||03-03-2001||07:14 PM||||I seem to remember seeing some kind of repair adhesive for urethane bumpers at Pep-Boys.||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||03-04-2001||10:18 AM||||If you're willing to take the chance that you won't be hitting anything with it, you can do a decent job patching your fascia with fiberglass and Bondo...patch the rear of the cracks with fiberglass and resin and fill in the front with Bondo.<P>Any repair you do will compromise the flexing ability of the fascia. I've patched two coupe fascias with glass and Bondo and you'd be hard pressed to tell they were fixed. And this method is probably cheaper than any other.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mbramble||03-04-2001||02:58 PM||||Well, I bought the 3M product for flexible panels at CarQuest yesterday.  $30...  But, it works great.  The tears are gone and the fascia still flexes as much as it did originally.  Great stuff.  All I need to do is to finish sanding it and prime/paint it.  We had major thunderstorms here last night and I got to this point just in time to get in before the weather started.  Not in time to get my PC unplugged and lost my modem.  Spent all morning shopping for a new one. <P>Thanks, as usual, for the info!! ||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||03-04-2001||05:09 PM||||that 3M stuff is great. It only cost $20 for the 2 tubes at my body supply. It is very flexible, but I guarantee if you hit it against something it will break back at same place. A Corvette guy didnt believe me till he parked in his garage...again.||||reg||1
